        "content": "<p>Aesop is great at solving many easy goals, such as</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Aesop</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">aesop</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>However, now let's try putting that into a set comprehension:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Aesop</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Data</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Defs</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">aesop</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"c1\">-- fails</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">aesop</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"c1\">-- also fails</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Let's trace to see what's going on:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">set_option</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">trace</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">aesop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">aesop</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>produces</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">Initial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">goal</span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">⊢</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">Goal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">after</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">normalisation</span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">⊢</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I'd like Aesop to normalize the target by expanding the expression to <code>∃z: Nat, 0 = z</code>, which (as we saw above) it should then be able to solve.</p>\n<p>Is there some way to ask Aesop to do this?  Ideally I'd like a solution that doesn't require me to mention S specifically - I'd like to ask it to reduce any target expression that is a set membership.</p>",
        "id": 616159648,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1786561246
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You could make <code>S</code> an <code>abbrev</code></p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">abbrev</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">aesop</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 616161576,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1786561937
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>True, but then S will be expanded everywhere it appears, which doesn't seem ideal since it could be inefficient.  Ideally I'd like to keep it as a def, but ask Aesop to expand it. </p>\n<p>In fact really I might like to add an unsafe low-priority rule to Aesop that tells it to expand any set comprehension, but only as a last resort after a proof without the expansion has not succeeded.  I'm not sure if that's possible, however.</p>",
        "id": 616163721,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1786562750
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Wait, there's more going on here than I realized.  Your example with a full <code>import Mathlib</code> works.  However if I just <code>import Mathlib.Data.Set.Defs</code> as in my example, it fails.  So apparently the full Mathlib import adds some implicit rules that are relevant.</p>",
        "id": 616164546,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1786563026
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>However, my original code still fails with a full Mathlib import.</p>",
